<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Congress of Angostura</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1819">1819</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Congress of Angostura was the assembly convened by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-bolivar">Simón Bolívar</a> at Angostura, now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ciudad-bolivar">Ciudad Bolívar</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orinoco">Orinoco</a>, which opened in February 1819. Bolívar's address set out his ideas on republican government, and the congress elected him president of Venezuela. In December 1819, after his victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boyaca-department">Boyacá</a>, it proclaimed the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombia">Colombia</a>, known as Gran Colombia, uniting Venezuela and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-granada">New Granada</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/congress-of-angostura.jpg" alt="Congress of Angostura" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Guillermo Ramos Flamerich · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:House_of_the_Congress_of_Angostura.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The assembly, also called the Second National Congress of Venezuela after the congress of 1811, met during the Venezuelan wars of independence as the congress of the re-established Republic of Venezuela. Much of the country was still under Spanish rule, so elections were held only in the parts of southern Venezuela and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/margarita-island">Margarita Island</a> held by the patriot forces. Twenty-six delegates represented six Venezuelan provinces; representatives of Casanare, the only liberated province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-granada">New Granada</a>, chosen from exiles fighting beside the Venezuelan patriots, joined later.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Not all of the proposals in Bolívar's Address at Angostura were accepted. The congress rejected his plan for an exalted ceremonial president for life governing through ministers answerable to parliament, a hereditary senate on the British model, and a fourth, moral branch of government loosely modelled on the Classical Areopagus. Its chief work was the Venezuelan Constitution of 1819, adopted on 15 August, which was soon made obsolete. Francisco Antonio Zea was chosen vice president, Juan Germán Roscio vice president for Venezuela and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-de-paula-santander">Francisco de Paula Santander</a> for Cundinamarca.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>The Fundamental Law of the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombia">Colombia</a>, enacted on 17 December 1819, had fourteen articles. It declared Venezuela and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-granada">New Granada</a> united in one republic out of necessity and mutual interest, joined their debts, and divided the state into three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">departments</a>, Venezuela, Cundinamarca and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quito">Quito</a>, with capitals at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caracas">Caracas</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogota">Bogotá</a> and Quito. A new capital was to bear Bolívar's name, the flag would take the Venezuelan colours, and a congress would meet at Rosario de Cúcuta in January 1821 to write a constitution. The republic was to be proclaimed with public celebrations on 25 December 1819.</p><p>Zea, as president of the congress, and the secretary Diego de Vallenilla signed the law, with deputies including Manuel Cedeño and Diego Bautista Urbaneja, and Bolívar approved its publication. Historians call the new state Gran Colombia to tell it apart from the present Republic of Colombia; its constitution was drawn up at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-cucuta">Congress of Cúcuta</a> in August 1821.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Congress of Angostura” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the collapse of the First and Second Venezuelan Republics, Simón Bolívar rebuilt his forces in the Orinoco basin, where the <b>Congress of Angostura</b> gathered in February 1819.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gran-colombia">Gran Colombia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Congress of Angostura</b> met in February 1819, when Bolívar delivered his famous address on government.</blockquote><a href="/state/third-republic-of-venezuela">Third Republic of Venezuela</a></li></ul></section>
